{"kind":"report","text":"Third independent review of notes/2026-08-13-second-application.md, at exact head 11633f05de97e7ef51cc5287f0957059befb10f7 (origin agrees; artifact 601395bc names this commit and is non-stale). My prior verdicts: f9d9f9b7 (changes-requested, ratified) and 802980ff (changes-requested, against 652dab99).\n\nGate: go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... at the exact head — ok, 87.789s, exit 0. Run in a detached worktree I created at 11633f05 and will remove, because the project worktree has moved on (see the head note below). Reported honestly again: internal/docset.Load walks docs/ only, so this gate never reads notes/ and cannot validate the changed file. It shows no docs regression; for a notes-only head that is a no-op with respect to the change.\n\nHEAD NOTE, not a finding against the design: the local branch has already advanced past the head under review to b66ced02 (\"Cover playing against an agent\", +26 lines), which is not pushed — origin is at 11633f05. My verdict binds to 11633f05 alone, and under the AGENTS.md rule that any change to the head invalidates the approval, a verdict on this head will not cover b66ced02 when it lands.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES-REQUESTED. All three blocking findings N1, N2 and N3 are closed, cleanly and exactly. The rewrite is a real improvement and I recommend keeping essentially all of it. But the rewrite dropped a load-bearing design constraint that the previous head carried, and that is precisely what this review was asked to catch. One restored clause and one corrected number would make this an approval; nothing else stands in the way.\n\n=== A. THE THREE BLOCKING FINDINGS ===\n\nN1 (deployer budget understated the deployment) — CLOSED, exactly as asked. The budget now reads: \"Create the data repository on a forge; clone the chess repository; set two secrets (sequencer key, forge push credential); launch; share the URL. Budget: one repository, two secrets, one launch command. GitHub login adds a provider registration, its client secret, and the witnessing key — three more setup steps.\" Both missing items — the forge push credential and the data-repository provisioning step — are now counted, and the login extras are itemized rather than gestured at. The body text agrees (\"set two secrets (the sequencer's signing key, and the credential that lets the container push to the forge)\"). This is the finding that drove two rounds; it is now correct in both places.\n\nN2 (persist() prompts in Firefox, breaking the zero-prompt path) — CLOSED, and better than my suggested fix. The custody bullet: \"the app asks for persistent storage *lazily* — after the first move has landed, or at anchor time — because that request can itself prompt in some browsers and the join path stays at zero prompts.\" And the deferral is stated in the budget itself, which is what I asked for: \"zero prompts before the first move — persistent-storage permission is deliberately deferred until after the first move so this stays true in every browser.\" The claim is now true in every browser, and the reason is on the page rather than in a reviewer's head.\n\nN3 (R1's capability chain neither adopted nor amended) — CLOSED, and handled better than by silence. It is now a named, deliberate amendment with its own bullet: \"The submit endpoint is open, by design... A public game server amends that scope deliberately: anyone may submit, because zero-setup play is the point, and record authority (the signature on every act) never depended on transport authorization anyway. The capability chain remains the rule for deployments that need authorization; a public application profile opts out on purpose and says so.\" The reasoning is right — record authority is the actor signature, which transport authorization never carried — and the amendment is scoped rather than global. The section header now says the design \"adopts — or explicitly amends —\" the invariants, which is the honest verb pair. Work item 6 carries it forward (\"the open submit intake as amended above\").\n\n=== B. THE REWRITE, JUDGED AS A REWRITE ===\n\nMethod: diffed 652dab99 against 11633f05 (405 insertions, 407 deletions on a 437-line file — effectively a total rewrite), compared section and bullet structure, and grepped both for the load-bearing constraint language rather than trusting a read-through.\n\nStructure is preserved and extended. Every section of 652dab99 survives, in the same order, with two new ones in front (Start here: running a game; What gitseq provides) and the fold-purity decision moved to a Provenance footer where it still rests. The anchor ladder is now an explicit two-axis grid with all three anchors classified on it, which is clearer than the prose it replaces.\n\nWHAT WAS LOST — L1, and this is the blocking finding.\n\nThe chess vocabulary lost its causal structure. 652dab99 read: \"`join` (rests on the create; carries the join secret when the create demands one; the first effective join seats the opponent), `move` (rests on the previous move or the join)\". 11633f05 reads: \"`join` (names the game; carries the join secret when the game demands one; the first join the fold accepts seats the opponent), `move`,\" — and `move` now carries no causal description at all. I grepped both for \"rests on\": it appears twice in each, but in 652dab99 both occurrences are these two vocabulary clauses, and in 11633f05 both are ordinary English elsewhere (\"that honesty rests on something unconditional\", \"this design rests on the ratified decision\"). The chess causal chain is gone from the document.\n\nThis is not a jargon simplification, it is a mechanism change. `rests_on` is the kernel's causal-reference field, carried in the signed intent and bounded there, and architecture.md layer 2 is explicit that the kernel carries those strings \"without assigning them application semantics\" — which is exactly the seam chess was built to demonstrate. \"Names the game\" describes a payload field instead: a payload reference does not enter the provenance graph, does not chain a move to its predecessor, and does not give the fold the ordering evidence that made \"wrong turn\" and \"second join\" detectable from the log's own structure. It also silently undercuts a live residual I raised last round — that on a rejected push the client must re-sign because the intent's rests_on names a predecessor a moved head may have invalidated. That analysis has no referent in this version.\n\nThe fix is one clause, and it need not be jargon: \"each move points at the one before it, and the first points at the join\" is plainer than what was there before and keeps the constraint. I would not have blocked on prose; I am blocking because a decision changed without being decided.\n\nL2 (blocking, but trivial — a number). The self-hoster budget now miscounts. It reads: \"Clone (or fork) the chess repository, build, `chess init`, `chess serve`, play on loopback. Budget: two commands after clone.\" Three commands are listed after clone: build, `chess init`, `chess serve`. 652dab99 said \"build and run: `chess init`\" — two, and consistent. The rewrite correctly added `chess serve` (the opening narrative needs it: \"She runs `chess init` in a fresh folder, then `chess serve`\") but did not update the count. Either say three, or say two commands after build. On its own this would not have blocked; it rides along with L1 because both are one-token fixes in the same file.\n\nWEAKENED BUT NOT LOST — non-blocking, worth naming so they are not lost next round:\n\nL3. \"It follows the tier-2 ladder in `notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md`, and adopts — or explicitly amends — that note's three tier-2 invariants.\" That note carries ten numbered requirements (R1 through R10) under its tiers-2-and-3 section; three is the number this design engages, not the number that exist. For the stated audience — a reader new to gitseq, who will not have read the deployment note — that sentence creates a false picture of the source. \"three of that note's tier-2 requirements\" fixes it.\n\nL4. The explicit statement that post-hoc anchoring is the normal path is thinned. 652dab99 said the attestation \"lands either as evidence on the join or as a free-standing `anchor` statement afterwards — the post-hoc path is the normal one, since play-first-anchor-later is the ordering the adoption story demands.\" That sentence was the direct resolution of my D3 finding. The rewrite keeps `anchor` in the vocabulary and the play-then-anchor ordering in the onboarding paths, so the design is intact and I am not reopening D3 — but the sentence that made the ordering a stated decision is gone, and the implementer of item 7 now infers it.\n\nL5. The deployment note requires a deployment to declare which storage topology it is (appliance-primary or forge-primary). 652dab99 named forge-primary as the chosen shape; the rewrite describes it conditionally (\"When the repository of record lives on a forge...\") without naming that a choice exists or was made. Minor, and arguably right for a plain-language note, but the declaration is a requirement of the note it claims to follow.\n\nIMPROVED BY THE REWRITE — recording these so they are not accidentally reverted:\n\nThe `internal/wireparity` citation is gone. That is a fix, not a loss: I found last round that wireparity compares JSON field *names* by regex and is the wrong precedent for pinning a canonical-CBOR encoder. The rewrite now says \"pinned by cross-tests against the Go implementation, and the kernel's byte-exact refusal of non-canonical input is the backstop\" — accurate, and it keeps the half that carries the safety. \"Kernel-verifiable but application-uninterpretable\" became \"verifiable but not interpretable\", which is plainer and means the same. The determinism rule moved up into \"What gitseq provides\", so a new reader meets it before it matters. \"Deterministic and total\" as a phrase is gone but both properties are stated in plain language; no loss.\n\n=== C. READABILITY FOR THE STATED AUDIENCE ===\n\nJudgement: it meets the condition, and by a clear margin. This is the strongest version of the document.\n\nThe Alice/Bob/Carol opening earns its place — six numbered steps that a reader can picture, ending on the sentence that actually explains why any of it matters (\"Push it to any host and the complete, verifiable history of every game travels with it\"). \"What gitseq provides\" does the hard part correctly: kernel and fold in one paragraph, with the illegal-move example doing the work that a definition would do worse (\"it sits in the history forever, changing nothing\"). Terms are bolded on first use and defined in place — kernel, application, fold, binding, anchor. Replacing R-numbers with plain names (\"One writer, enforced\"; \"Acknowledge after the push\"; \"The submit endpoint is open, by design\") is a real gain: the previous version required holding a second document in your head to read a bullet list. Dropping the review-history narration is right — a design note should read as a design, not as a changelog of its own review, and the previous head opened by explaining itself to its reviewer rather than to its reader.\n\nTwo readability notes, neither blocking. \"Verifiable but not interpretable\" is introduced and explained but the reader is never told what to do in that state; the earlier version tied it to a degraded state the architecture page defines. And the live layer is now \"the game's live channel\" rather than the nexus — better for a newcomer, but the architecture reference calls it the nexus, so the implementer of item 5 has one unmapped term between this note and the page it must satisfy. A parenthetical on first use would cost nothing.\n\n=== D. FRICTION TRACE, RE-WALKED AGAINST THE CURRENT BUDGETS ===\n\n1. SPECTATOR. Budget: two steps, no key, no prompt; cold state named; watcher-list/chat mints silently. Walk: receive watch URL, open, board renders. 2 steps, 0 prompts, 0 installs, 0 redirects, 0 copy-paste. HONEST. The watch link \"simply omits\" the join secret, so the spectator and player links are properly distinct.\n\n2. ANONYMOUS PLAYER. Budget: zero prompts before the first move, with the persistent-storage deferral stated. Walk: receive invitation URL, open (key minted silently into IndexedDB, no persistence request yet), tap join carrying the fragment secret, move; persistence requested only after the move lands. 2 user actions to the join, 0 prompts, 0 installs, 0 redirects, 0 copy-paste. HONEST IN EVERY BROWSER now — N2's fix is what makes that true. Nothing removable: the join tap still has to stay, because it is what seats the opponent. Silent failures: storage eviction remains, and is named on the page with anchoring offered as the durable fix.\n\n3. ANCHORED PLAYER, GITHUB LOGIN. Budget: one redirect round-trip, one first-time consent screen, zero installs, zero copy-paste. Walk matches; callback returns to the game origin, so the browser key survives the round-trip. HONEST.\n\n4. ANCHORED PLAYER, NOSTR. Budget: two extension prompts for users who already run NIP-07; \"for anyone else, honestly: an extension install and a key backup first\". Never required to play. HONEST, and still the best-stated budget in the document because it names the population it applies to.\n\n5. AGENT CREDENTIAL. Budget: three manual steps, measured at acceptance. Walk: mint agent key, endorse it, hand the agent its key and the repository. Three verbs, three steps. Still uncounted: the endorsement prompt (one extension prompt on the Nostr anchor) and the agent runtime itself. HONEST ENOUGH, unchanged from my last assessment — it is falsifiable and item 8 is scheduled to measure it; just do not let the measurement quietly exclude those two.\n\n6. DEPLOYER. Budget: one repository, two secrets, one launch command, with login extras itemized. Walk: create data repository on the forge, clone chess, set sequencer key, set forge push credential, launch, share URL. Every item now appears in the count. HONEST — this is the finding closed.\n\n7. SELF-HOSTER. Budget: two commands after clone. Walk: build, `chess init`, `chess serve` — three. NOT HONEST, by one, and newly so. See L2.\n\nTally: six honest (five outright, one honest-enough), one off by one. The two budgets that had been wrong for two rounds are now right; the error moved to the one path that had been correct.\n\n=== E. THE THREE CONCLUSIONS ===\n\nARCHITECTURE. Layers touched, unchanged from my last review: 2 (kernel, correctly unchanged); 3 (the live layer, used exactly as the contract defines — short-lived leases, dies with the session, never pretends to be a record); 4 (application profiles become plural, gain a selection rule, and gain a host-level binding vocabulary); 5 (read the binding, select, then fold; bounded projections; two separately labeled cursors); 6 (surfaces — one binary shipping kernel, fold, projections, service and UI). This head is notes-only and changes no contract today. Work item 1 still commits the contract change and its architecture-reference row to one head, which satisfies the review rule prospectively. One architecture-adjacent consequence of L1: dropping the causal chain from the vocabulary weakens the demonstration that an application can express its own causality through the kernel's opaque `rests_on` strings, which is the layer-2/layer-4 seam this whole design exists to prove. That is the deeper reason L1 blocks rather than merely annoys.\n\nSECURITY. Boundaries examined: binding authority and positional effectiveness, the two-axis anchor ladder, witnessing-key durability, browser key custody and the persistence deferral, seat ownership and recovery, agent credential minting, the now-amended open submit endpoint, and forge-primary write custody. Everything closed in my previous two reviews stays closed at this head, and I re-checked each rather than assuming: log-internal time and no network in the fold; writer lease and acknowledge-after-push; non-extractable keys in IndexedDB with the eviction hazard named; the witness key recorded in the log so bindings outlive the deployment; the standing forgery capability named and bounded by sequencing. N3's amendment is sound: opening the submit endpoint does not weaken record authority, because authority was never transport-derived, and the note says exactly that. Outstanding and unchanged: the dual-signature requirement on `anchor` is still only implied; motion-hint authorship is still unstated, so a watcher could animate a piece on other watchers' boards; chat abuse on a public live channel is still unaddressed while volume abuse on submit is. None is a hole in the ladder.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The rewrite is itself the simplification pass, and a good one: two documents' worth of cross-referencing collapsed into plain names, the wrong wireparity precedent dropped, the review-history narration removed, and the ladder turned into a grid. What remains, unchanged from last round: the invitation still carries two mechanisms where only the join-secret variant can serve the anonymous path, since a creator cannot know a key that is minted when the invitee opens the page — say which serves which, or drop one. And L1 is the counter-case worth stating plainly: this pass cut one thing that was carrying weight. The eight work items remain well factored, and item 5 still points at the complete-application section as its specification rather than restating it.\n\n=== F. WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS AN APPROVAL ===\nRestore the causal clauses to the vocabulary — `join` rests on the create, `move` rests on the previous move or the join, in whatever plain wording fits the rewrite — and correct the self-hoster budget to three commands after clone (or two after build). L3, L4 and L5 are worth folding in while the file is open, but I would not hold the head for them. Everything else in this version I endorse: the three closed findings, the whole plain-language treatment, the two-axis grid, and every core decision carried forward from the two heads before it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:601395bc928828a31153bc8f9e8cd3817d12f3df","head":"11633f05de97e7ef51cc5287f0957059befb10f7","verdict":"changes-requested"}}